@startuml
' ============================================================================
' METADATA
'   author:       TheRobotDrafts Examples
'   description:  Medium Yourdon-style Data Flow Diagram for a bank deposit
'                 subsystem: a context diagram plus a Level-0 DFD with 5
'                 processes, multiple stores and externals, and flows between
'                 processes.
'   usecase:      Teaching DFD leveling in practice — how a context diagram
'                 (one process) explodes into a Level-0 DFD (many processes)
'                 while keeping input/output balance, and inter-process flows.
'   complexity:   medium
'   domain:       banking / deposit processing
' ============================================================================

title Data Flow Diagram - Medium Bank (Context + Level-0)

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' TEACHING NOTES
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' This file contains TWO leveled DFDs for the SAME system:
'   - CONTEXT diagram: the whole system as ONE process (bubble 0) plus externals.
'   - LEVEL-0 diagram: bubble 0 EXPLODED into 5 processes (1..5), with stores
'     and inter-process flows.
'
' LEVELING / BALANCE rule (critical):
'   The flows crossing the SYSTEM BOUNDARY must be IDENTICAL at every level.
'   In the context diagram Customer->System carries "deposit slip" and "cash";
'   in the Level-0 those same two flows must enter some process (here process 1).
'   The Statement->System "deposit notice" likewise must appear. If you add or
'   drop a flow when exploding, the diagrams are out of balance.
'
' Numbering convention:
'   Context bubble   = 0
'   Level-0 bubbles  = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5
'   Level-1 of proc 3 = 3.1, 3.2, 3.3 (each explodes ONE level-0 bubble further)
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

' ============================
' CONTEXT DIAGRAM (Level -1 / 0)
' ============================
package "Context Diagram" {
  rectangle "Customer"    as C1 <<externalEntity>>
  rectangle "Statement\nSys" as S1 <<externalEntity>>

  usecase "0\nDeposit\nSystem" as Sys0 <<process>>

  C1 --> Sys0 : deposit slip
  C1 --> Sys0 : cash
  Sys0 --> S1 : deposit notice
  Sys0 --> C1 : receipt
}

' ============================
' LEVEL-0 DIAGRAM (explode bubble 0)
' ============================
package "Level-0 Diagram" {
  ' Externals reappear (same boundary flows as context).
  rectangle "Customer"     as C2 <<externalEntity>>
  rectangle "Statement\nSys" as S2 <<externalEntity>>

  ' Five processes
  usecase "1\nReceive\nDeposit"  as P1 <<process>>
  usecase "2\nValidate"          as P2 <<process>>
  usecase "3\nPost to\nAccount"  as P3 <<process>>
  usecase "4\nUpdate\nBalance"   as P4 <<process>>
  usecase "5\nIssue\nReceipt"    as P5 <<process>>

  ' Data stores (internal)
  storage "Accounts\n(D1)" as D1 <<dataStore>>
  storage "Deposit\nLog (D2)" as D2 <<dataStore>>

  ' --- Boundary flows (must match context diagram) ---
  C2 --> P1 : deposit slip
  C2 --> P1 : cash
  P5 --> S2 : deposit notice
  P5 --> C2 : receipt

  ' --- Inter-process flows ---
  P1 --> P2 : raw deposit
  P2 --> P3 : valid deposit
  P3 --> P4 : posting
  P3 --> P5 : posted deposit

  ' --- Process <-> store flows ---
  P2 --> D2 : validation event
  P3 --> D1 : account update
  P3 --> D2 : deposit record
  D1 --> P4 : current balance
  P4 --> D1 : new balance
  D2 --> P5 : log entry
}

note bottom of P3
  **Leveling explained**
  CONTEXT: system = one bubble, only externals + boundary flows.
  LEVEL-0: explode that bubble into major processes; SAME boundary
           flows cross in/out; add INTERNAL stores and inter-process flows.
  LEVEL-1: explode any ONE level-0 bubble further (e.g. 3 -> 3.1, 3.2).
  --
  Balance check for THIS diagram:
   context boundary = {deposit slip, cash, deposit notice, receipt}
   level-0 boundary = {deposit slip, cash, deposit notice, receipt}  MATCH
end note

note right of D1
  **Stores are internal**
  Data stores appear at Level-0 and below,
  NEVER in the context diagram (they are
  inside the system boundary).
end note
@enduml
